The ultimate homemade upgrade to boxed hamburger helpers. Tender elbow macaroni is enveloped in a four-cheese sauce made from cheddar, gouda, parmesan, and American cheese, spiked with classic burger condiments—ketchup, ground mustard, and paprika. Tossed with seasoned ground beef and finished with pickle relish and smoky bacon bits.

## Ingredients

### Ground Beef & Aromatics
- 1 tablespoon unsalted butter
- 12 oz lean ground beef (85% or 90% lean)
- ½ cup finely diced white onion, divided (¼ cup cooked with beef, ¼ cup kept crisp for finish)
- ½ teaspoon kosher salt
- ¼ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper

### Pasta & Burger Cheese Sauce
- 10 oz uncooked elbow macaroni (about 2 ½ cups)
- 1 tablespoon unsalted butter
- 2 tablespoons all-purpose flour
- 2 cups cold whole milk
- 2 tablespoons tomato ketchup
- 1 ½ teaspoons dry ground mustard (or yellow prepared mustard)
- ¾ teaspoon sweet paprika
- 1 cup shredded sharp cheddar cheese
- ¼ cup shredded young gouda cheese
- ¼ cup freshly grated parmesan cheese
- 2 slices American cheese, torn into pieces (ensures a glossy, un-broken cheese sauce)

### Optional Burger Garnishes
- 3 tablespoons sweet pickle relish or diced dill pickles
- ¼ cup crisp crumbled bacon bits

## Instructions

1. **Brown the Beef Base:** In a large Dutch oven or heavy-bottomed pasta pot, melt 1 tablespoon of butter over medium-high heat. Add the ground beef, ¼ cup of the diced white onion, ½ teaspoon kosher salt, and ¼ teaspoon black pepper. Cook for 5 to 6 minutes, chopping the beef with a wooden spoon into small crumbles, until browned and cooked through. Transfer the cooked beef and onions to a plate with a slotted spoon; set aside.
2. **Par-Boil the Macaroni:** Fill the same pot with water, add a generous pinch of salt, and bring to a rapid boil. Drop in the elbow macaroni and cook for about 5 to 6 minutes—just halfway cooked so the pasta still has a firm bite in the center. Carefully dip a measuring cup into the pot to scoop out 1 cup of hot starchy pasta cooking water. Drain the pasta in a colander and set aside.
3. **Build the Sauce Roux:** Place the empty pot back over medium heat and melt the remaining 1 tablespoon of butter. Sprinkle in 2 tablespoons of all-purpose flour. Whisk continuously for 60 seconds to toast the flour and eliminate any raw paste taste.
4. **Whisk the Burger Sauce:** Gradually pour in the 2 cups of whole milk in a steady stream while whisking vigorously to avoid lumps. Whisk in the ketchup, dry mustard powder, and sweet paprika. Bring the sauce to a gentle simmer, whisking constantly along the bottom, for 2 to 3 minutes until it thickens into a creamy sauce that blankets a spoon.
5. **Melt the Four Cheeses:** Lower the stovetop heat to low. Add the shredded cheddar, gouda, parmesan, and the pieces of American cheese. Stir gently with a silicone spatula until all the cheese has melted into a silky, golden, velvety sauce. If the sauce looks too thick, drizzle in 2 to 4 tablespoons of the reserved warm pasta water to loosen it to a luscious ribbon texture.
6. **Fold and Simmer:** Dump the drained macaroni, the cooked ground beef, and the remaining ¼ cup of crisp raw diced white onions into the cheese sauce. Simmer gently over low heat for 2 to 3 minutes, stirring constantly, so the pasta finishes cooking in the sauce and absorbs all the savory burger flavor.
7. **Garnish and Serve:** Ladle steaming bowls immediately. Crown each portion with a spoonful of sweet or dill pickle relish and a sprinkle of crispy bacon bits for that authentic drive-thru diner flavor profile.

## Notes

- **The American Cheese Secret:** American cheese contains sodium citrate, an emulsifier that guarantees a completely smooth, restaurant-grade cheese sauce that will never curdle or separate into grease.
- **Leftovers & Reheating:** Keeps in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 4 days. When reheating on the stovetop or in the microwave, add a splash of milk or broth to loosen the cheese sauce.
